      "topic": "Logarithmic barrier and global truncations",
      "result": "PASS",
      "details": "The smooth convex truncations are decreasing in the truncation index, constant near the divisor, and retain nonnegative first and second derivatives. A sufficiently large norm shift bounds the smooth background between fixed positive multiples of the K\u00e4hler form."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Rank-one energy calculation at crossings",
      "result": "PASS",
      "details": "The gradient wedge eliminates the rank-one Hessian term. Integration by parts has the stated positive gradient sign. Each singular radial term integrates as integral_0^infinity(a+s)^(-2)ds=1/a; other radial variables carry exponential volume factors."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Finite energy and full mass",
      "result": "PASS",
      "details": "The uniform negative-energy bound survives decreasing approximation, and energy monotonicity transfers it to the original potential. BBGZ Proposition2.8 and BEGZ Proposition2.11 give full mass and integrability. Normalized reference energies may be multiplied by the positive volume to obtain the stated unnormalized functional."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Recovery of every original mixed mass",
      "result": "PASS",
      "details": "Actual local C2 convergence, not L1 alone, justifies fixed-epsilon Fatou. Every mixed deficit is nonnegative. Full mass in one positive corrected class gives a positive-coefficient polynomial identity whose deficits must all vanish, including when the original nef top self-intersection is zero."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Original approximants and corrected energy",
      "result": "PASS",
      "details": "For each fixed positive correction choose a smooth positive representative. The source gives approximation from above and a common upper bound off the divisor; continuity and upper-semicontinuous extension give the global comparison. The logarithmic lower bound supplies L1 domination. Monotonicity and upper semicontinuity sandwich the energies, without monotonicity in the source parameter."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Signed energy coefficient recovery",
      "result": "PASS",
      "details": "All weighted mixed coefficients are absolutely integrable by the positive corrected Monge-Ampere energy. Both smooth and limiting energy expressions are polynomials of degree at most n in a fixed large correction. Values at n+1 fixed large parameters recover the constant coefficient. No exchange of the original regularization limit with a vanishing K\u00e4hler correction is used."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Concavity sign and exact cusp coefficient",
      "result": "PASS with stated source interfaces open",
      "details": "After the requested notation clarification, formula14 uses E_z and the corrected current theta+z omega+ddc phi. Concavity gives E_z(psi)-E_z(phi) at most the integral of psi-phi against the phi measure. The actual source cuspCorr is phMu+cuspWeight/2, so the conditional final coefficient is exactly (n+1)(a-meanU/2)degMu."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Scope and missing arithmetic interfaces",
